## rayguix.pengu
## Layer-2 wrapper over the 38 raygui functions worth wrapping, out of the 61 the binding declares.
##
## ## One deliberate omission worth knowing about
## `GuiLoadStyleFromMemory` is **not** wrapped, even though it takes a slice. Measured: raygui
## reads a `.rgs` buffer **without validating its length**, and a buffer whose first four bytes
## are the valid magic `rGS ` but which is too short segfaults the process. A wrapper cannot
## validate a style file, so this one stays at the binding, where the caller passes
## `ref to frozen byte, dataSize` knowingly. `load_style`, which reads a file, **is** wrapped,
## because it was measured to fail safely on a missing path.
##
## raygui is a widget library, so almost everything here takes a `const char*` and returns
## raygui's own state:
##
##     var c_text as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with "Save"
##     defer calling ffi.cstr_free with (transmute c_text to ref to char)
##     var state as int is calling raygui.GuiButton with bounds, c_text    # binding
##
##     var state as int is calling rayguix.button with bounds, "Save"      # wrapper
##
## ## Why the module can exist at all
## raygui is a single-header library: its implementation only exists in a translation unit
## that defines `RAYGUI_IMPLEMENTATION`. That is already handled by `c/raygui_impl.c`, which the
## build compiles into the bundle, so no `antiquus`, no compiler change and no extra archive are
## needed -- and measured, the `Gui*` symbols are absent from `libraylib.so`, which is why the
## bridge is load-bearing rather than decorative. See `docs/plans/rayguix.md`.
##
## ## The out-parameter shape
## Twenty-two widgets read **and write** a value the caller owns -- a toggle's flag, a slider's
## number, a picker's colour. The wrapper keeps the binding's shape instead of inventing a
## return value: the call site passes `sigil of enabled`, and the widget's own return stays what
## raygui means by it, the drawing state.
##
## ## The three text fields
## `text_box`, `value_box_float` and `text_input_box` edit a caller-owned C buffer in place, and
## PenguScript strings are immutable. The wrapper therefore allocates the buffer, seeds it from
## your string, calls raygui and copies the edited text back into the `ref to string` you
## passed; `capacity` is the buffer's size, and typing past it truncates rather than overflows.
## The copy is byte by byte because the language has no `memcpy`, no pointer arithmetic and no
## assignable slice view.
##
## ## Needs a window
## Every widget draws, so every test opens one. raygui keeps its state in globals that survive
## `CloseWindow`, so the tests use the binding's own `GuiSetStyle`/`GuiGetStyle` to save and
## restore anything they change.
##
## ## Memory
## Nothing is leaked: the temporary C strings and the text-field buffers are released by the
## wrapper, and the caller's values stay the caller's.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## import raylib
## import rayguix
##
## weave main into int:
##     var volume as f32 is 0.5
##     var bounds as raylib.Rectangle is with x is 20.0, y is 20.0, width is 200.0, height is 24.0
##     var state as int is calling rayguix.slider with bounds, "0", "100", (sigil of volume), 0.0, 100.0
##     return 0
## ```


import raylib
import raygui
import std.ffi
import std.ward

## Zeroes `capacity` bytes of a caller-owned buffer. Private to the module.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `buffer`: the byte view to clear.
## - `capacity`: how many bytes to write.
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing.
##
## ## Notes
## raygui reads its text buffer as a C string, so a field that starts as `MemAlloc` garbage would
## print whatever happened to be in the heap. This is why the buffer is cleared before seeding.
##
## ## Memory
## The buffer belongs to the caller.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling _clear_buffer with buffer, capacity
## ```
weave _clear_buffer with buffer as slice of byte, capacity as int into void:
    var i as int is 0
    while i < capacity:
        set essence of (sigil of (buffer at i)) is (0 to byte)
        set i is i + 1

## Copies a PenguScript string into a caller-owned buffer and NUL-terminates it. Private.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `buffer`: the byte view to write into.
## - `text`: the text to copy.
## - `capacity`: the buffer's size in bytes.
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing.
##
## ## Notes
## The copy is byte by byte: the language has no `memcpy`, no pointer arithmetic and no
## assignable slice view, so this loop is the only expressible route. It stops at the string's
## length and leaves the rest of the buffer cleared, which is what raygui expects from an
## editable field.
##
## ## Memory
## The buffer and the string both belong to the caller; the temporary C string of the source is
## freed here.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling _seed_buffer with buffer, "editable", capacity
## ```
weave _seed_buffer with buffer as slice of byte, text as string, capacity as int into void:
    calling _clear_buffer with buffer, capacity
    let c_text as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with text
    defer calling ffi.cstr_free with (transmute c_text to ref to char)
    let source_raw as ref to void is transmute c_text to ref to void
    var source as slice of byte is calling ffi.slice_of_bytes_from_ptr with source_raw, ((text.length) + 1)
    var i as int is 0
    while i < (text.length):
        set essence of (sigil of (buffer at i)) is (source at i)
        set i is i + 1

## Reads a NUL-terminated string out of a caller-owned buffer. Private.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `buffer`: the byte view to read from.
##
## ## Returns
## The text raygui left in the buffer, as an owned PenguScript string.
##
## ## Notes
## raygui edits the buffer in place, so this is how the edited text gets back into the caller's
## `string`.
##
## ## Memory
## The returned string is owned by the caller of the wrapper; the buffer stays the wrapper's.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## set essence of text is (calling _read_buffer with buffer)
## ```
weave _read_buffer with buffer as slice of byte into string:
    let raw as ref to void is transmute (sigil of (buffer at 0)) to (ref to void)
    return calling ffi.string_from_cstr with (transmute raw to ref to char)


## Loads a visual style from a file.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `path`: the `.rgs` style file to read
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing.
##
## ## Notes
## raygui ships its stock styles as `.rgs` files, and a missing one leaves the current style untouched while raygui logs the failure: **the binding reports nothing**, so the docstring says so rather than pretending.
## The style is global: it applies to every widget drawn afterwards.
##
## ## Memory
## Each PenguScript string is copied into a C string that the wrapper frees before returning; raygui keeps nothing.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling rayguix.load_style with "dark.rgs"
## ```

weave load_style with path as string into void:
    let c_path as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with path
    defer calling ffi.cstr_free with (transmute c_path to ref to char)
    calling raygui.GuiLoadStyle with c_path

## Sets the text the next widget shows as a tooltip.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `text`: the tooltip text
##
## ## Returns
## Nothing.
##
## ## Notes
## raygui shows the tooltip on hover; calling this with an empty string clears it.
##
## ## Memory
## Each PenguScript string is copied into a C string that the wrapper frees before returning; raygui keeps nothing.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling rayguix.set_tooltip with "Save the file"
## ```

weave set_tooltip with text as string into void:
    let c_text as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with text
    defer calling ffi.cstr_free with (transmute c_text to ref to char)
    calling raygui.GuiSetTooltip with c_text

## Measures a string in the current GUI font.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `text`: the text to measure
##
## ## Returns
## raygui's widget state as an `int`: measured, `0` is idle and `1` is the press or change signal, so a click test is `state == 1`. The wrapper does not translate it into a `bool` because raygui distinguishes more states than two.
##
## ## Notes
## This is how a layout computes the width a label needs before placing it.
## **Measured trap:** raygui keeps its font in a process-global and initialises it lazily,
## only once. A program that closes a window and opens another must rebind it with
## `GuiSetFont(GetFontDefault())`, otherwise this measures `0` and every widget draws its
## text with a dead texture. The tests do exactly that after each `InitWindow`.
## It measures the **GUI font**, not raylib's default font, so it is the measurement that matches what the widgets will draw.
##
## ## Memory
## Each PenguScript string is copied into a C string that the wrapper frees before returning; raygui keeps nothing.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var w as int is calling rayguix.text_width with "Save"
## ```

weave text_width with text as string into int:
    let c_text as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with text
    defer calling ffi.cstr_free with (transmute c_text to ref to char)
    return calling raygui.GuiGetTextWidth with c_text

## Builds the icon-plus-text label raygui's widgets expect.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `icon_id`: which icon from the icon set
## - `text`: the text to put beside the icon
##
## ## Returns
## An owned PenguScript `string` holding the built label.
##
## ## Notes
## raygui has no rich text: passing this string to a widget is how an icon gets drawn inside a button or a label.
## **Measured: raygui returns a static internal buffer**, so the second call overwrites the first. The wrapper copies it into an owned string immediately, which is why the result is safe to hold.
##
## ## Memory
## Each PenguScript string is copied into a C string that the wrapper frees before returning; raygui keeps nothing.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## var label as string is calling rayguix.icon_text with 1, "Save"
## ```

weave icon_text with icon_id as int, text as string into string:
    let c_text as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with text
    defer calling ffi.cstr_free with (transmute c_text to ref to char)
    let c_result as ref to frozen char is calling raygui.GuiIconText with icon_id, c_text
    return calling ffi.string_from_cstr with (transmute c_result to ref to char)

## Draws a window frame with a title.
##
## ## Parameters
## - `bounds`: the widget's rectangle in screen coordinates
## - `title`: the frame's title
##
## ## Returns
## raygui's widget state as an `int`: measured, `0` is idle and `1` is the press or change signal, so a click test is `state == 1`. The wrapper does not translate it into a `bool` because raygui distinguishes more states than two.
##
## ## Notes
## It is the container every other widget sits in; the title is drawn in the frame's caption bar.
## Returns raygui's state for the frame: `0` is idle, and the close button reports `1` when clicked.
##
## ## Memory
## Each PenguScript string is copied into a C string that the wrapper frees before returning; raygui keeps nothing.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling rayguix.window_box with bounds, "Settings"
## ```

weave window_box with bounds as raylib.Rectangle, title as string into int:
    let c_title as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with title
    defer calling ffi.cstr_free with (transmute c_title to ref to char)
    return calling raygui.GuiWindowBox with bounds, c_title

