## - `capacity`: the field's buffer size in bytes
## - `edit_mode`: whether the field is being typed into
##
## ## Returns
## raygui's widget state as an `int`: measured, `0` is idle and `1` is the press or change signal, so a click test is `state == 1`. The wrapper does not translate it into a `bool` because raygui distinguishes more states than two.
##
## ## Notes
## Two outputs from one widget: raygui keeps the text and the parsed value, and the caller gets both through references.
## `label` is the `const char*` drawn beside the field; the editable text lives in the buffer the wrapper manages.
##
## ## Memory
## The wrapper owns the C buffer and frees it; the caller's `string` is replaced by an owned copy of the edited text.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## calling rayguix.value_box_float with bounds, "Gain", (sigil of text), (sigil of gain), 32, editing
## ```

weave value_box_float with bounds as raylib.Rectangle, label as string, text as ref to string, value as ref to f32, capacity as int, edit_mode as bool into int:
    let c_label as ref to frozen char is calling ffi.cstr_from_string with label
    defer calling ffi.cstr_free with (transmute c_label to ref to char)
    let raw as ref to void is calling raylib.MemAlloc with (capacity to u32)
    defer calling raylib.MemFree with raw
    var buffer as slice of byte is calling ffi.slice_of_bytes_from_ptr with raw, capacity
    calling _seed_buffer with buffer, (essence of text), capacity
    var state as int is calling raygui.GuiValueBoxFloat with bounds, c_label, (transmute raw to ref to char), value, edit_mode
    set essence of text is (calling _read_buffer with buffer)
    return state

